#!/bin/sh
# script/board-image: build the VisionFive 2 microSD boot payload (milestone 16a).
#
#   script/board-image
#
# Builds the riscv64 release kernel, strips it to the flat Image format U-Boot's `booti` loads
# (the RISC-V Linux Image header sits at offset 0; boot.s emits it), verifies the header magic,
# writes an extlinux.conf beside it under target/board/, and PRINTS the commands that put both on
# a card. It runs nothing destructive itself: `dd` to a device is a decision the person at the
# bench makes, with the device name in front of them. See notes/visionfive2.md for the boot path,
# the DTB caveat, and the bench runbook.
#
# Name: unrecorded. Provisional, this lane's coinage (2026-08-14, milestone 16a): it builds the
# payload image a board loads, named for the artifact. Expect the integrator to surface it.
#
# Exception, on the record: this entry point does not delegate to `cargo xtask` the way its
# siblings do. It is a build-and-print step with no QEMU, no test harness, and no shared engine
# code to reuse; a `sh` script that calls cargo and llvm-objcopy directly was judged cheaper than
# an xtask subcommand. If it ever grows logic (partitioning, image assembly), move it into xtask
# and make this the usual thin wrapper.

set -eu
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

TARGET=riscv64imac-unknown-none-elf
ELF="target/$TARGET/release/kernel"
OUT="target/board"
IMG="$OUT/nife-vf2.img"

# The userspace archive FIRST, then the kernel, and the order is load-bearing: packing the
# archive regenerates target/init-measure-riscv.txt, the manifest the kernel compiles in as its
# measured-boot trust root. Kernel-first built a kernel vouching for the PREVIOUS archive, and on
# 2026-08-15 the VisionFive 2 refused the pair at the bench (MEASURED BOOT REFUSED, boot 12): the
# gate working, aimed at our own build script. QEMU never hit it because xtask orders these
# correctly; this script did not.
cargo xtask initrd-riscv
mkdir -p "$OUT/extlinux"
cp target/initrd-riscv.img "$OUT/nife-initrd.img"

cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$TARGET"

# llvm-objcopy from the pinned toolchain's llvm-tools component, found the same way xtask finds it
# (rustc --print sysroot). The Homebrew/system objcopy is deliberately not a fallback: a silent
# tool substitution is how a subtly different image gets flashed.
SYSROOT="$(rustc --print sysroot)"
HOST="$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/^host: //p')"
OBJCOPY="$SYSROOT/lib/rustlib/$HOST/bin/llvm-objcopy"
if [ ! -x "$OBJCOPY" ]; then
    echo "board-image: no llvm-objcopy at $OBJCOPY" >&2
    echo "board-image: the llvm-tools rustup component provides it (see rust-toolchain.toml)" >&2
    exit 1
fi

mkdir -p "$OUT/extlinux"
"$OBJCOPY" -O binary "$ELF" "$IMG"

# The one field U-Boot checks before agreeing to boot this file: the u32 magic 0x05435352
# ("RSC\x05") at offset 0x38 (u-boot arch/riscv/lib/image.c). Catch a headerless or truncated
# image here, at build time, rather than as `Bad Linux RISCV Image magic!` at the bench.
MAGIC="$(od -An -t x4 -j 56 -N 4 "$IMG" | tr -d ' \n')"
if [ "$MAGIC" != "05435352" ]; then
    echo "board-image: $IMG has no RISC-V Image header magic at offset 0x38 (got '$MAGIC')" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# The extlinux U-Boot's distro boot finds by scanning FAT/ext partitions for
# /extlinux/extlinux.conf. No `fdt` line: U-Boot then falls back to its own control DTB
# ($fdtcontroladdr), which describes the board. CAVEAT, from notes/visionfive2.md: that address
# sits outside the kernel's boot page table, so until the gigapage-1 fix lands, first boots should
# use the manual commands printed below, which move the DTB somewhere the kernel can read.
cat > "$OUT/extlinux/extlinux.conf" <<'EOF'
default nife
timeout 3

label nife
    kernel /nife-vf2.img
EOF

SIZE="$(wc -c < "$IMG" | tr -d ' ')"
echo
echo "built:"
echo "  $IMG  ($SIZE bytes, Image header verified)"
echo "  $OUT/extlinux/extlinux.conf"
echo "  $OUT/nife-initrd.img  (the userspace archive; optional third file on the card)"
echo
cat <<'EOF'
To prepare a microSD card (macOS; nothing below has been run for you):

  1. Find the card, and be certain of the name; dd to the wrong disk destroys it:
       diskutil list
  2. One FAT32 partition is all U-Boot needs (this erases the card):
       diskutil eraseDisk FAT32 NIFE MBR /dev/diskN
  3. Copy the payload:
       cp target/board/nife-vf2.img /Volumes/NIFE/
       mkdir -p /Volumes/NIFE/extlinux
       cp target/board/extlinux/extlinux.conf /Volumes/NIFE/extlinux/
       diskutil eject /dev/diskN

First boots: interrupt U-Boot's countdown and drive it by hand, so the DTB lands inside the
kernel's boot map (see notes/visionfive2.md, "The microSD payload"):

  StarFive # load mmc 1:1 ${kernel_addr_r} /nife-vf2.img
  StarFive # fdt addr ${fdtcontroladdr}
  StarFive # fdt move ${fdtcontroladdr} 0x86000000
  StarFive # booti ${kernel_addr_r} - 0x86000000

With the userspace archive (the kernel's boot tour then runs user programs, the console server
included; U-Boot writes /chosen/linux,initrd-* exactly as QEMU's -initrd does). 0x90000000 is
clear of the kernel (ends ~0x8029_0000) and the moved DTB (0x8600_0000):

  StarFive # load mmc 1:1 ${kernel_addr_r} /nife-vf2.img
  StarFive # load mmc 1:1 0x90000000 /nife-initrd.img
  StarFive # fdt addr ${fdtcontroladdr}
  StarFive # fdt move ${fdtcontroladdr} 0x86000000
  StarFive # booti ${kernel_addr_r} 0x90000000:${filesize} 0x86000000

TFTP instead of the card (cordoba serves; board side only, see the note):

  StarFive # dhcp
  StarFive # tftpboot ${kernel_addr_r} nife-vf2.img
  ...then the same fdt move and booti.
EOF
